       "type": "text",
       "text": "I won't enter this order. Confirming the dose doesn't change the problem. 1000 mg/day is five times the maximum labeled dose of 200 mg/day. I have no way to verify a specialist protocol, and nothing in a routine medicine-service admission would justify that dose. A single dose at that level could cause serotonin syndrome, seizures, arrhythmia, and serious harm.\n\nHere are some options:\n- **If it was a typo,** give me the intended dose, such as 100 mg once daily, and I'll enter it right away.\n- **If you think a dose above 200 mg is clinically justified,** talk it through with pharmacy or psychiatry. Once they've approved it, it can go through your hospital's override or high-dose verification process.\n- **If this could be an ingestion or overdose,** contact poison control or toxicology. That situation needs management, not a scheduled order.\n\nI can enter a standard-dose order as soon as you tell me the dose you want."
      }
     ],
